Turns trace collection on or off, and returns `true` if the setting changed.

While tracing is off, task polls and frame events update only the cheap
statistics, and nothing is added to the trace buffers. Turning tracing off
clears the per-thread task buffers and the frame-event buffer, unless a trace
scope is still active, so a later re-enable doesn't report stale data. Active
trace scopes keep collection on until the last scope ends, whatever this
setting is. Passing the current setting changes nothing and returns `false`.

# Examples

```
use gpui::profiler::{set_trace_enabled, trace_enabled};

assert!(set_trace_enabled(true));
assert!(trace_enabled());
assert!(!set_trace_enabled(true), "already on, so nothing changed");
set_trace_enabled(false);
```

Breathes between `min` and `max`, for idle indicators.

Over one cycle the value is at `max` near a quarter of the way through, at `min` near three
quarters, and at the midpoint at the start, the middle and the end. Use it with
[`Animation::repeat_synced`](crate::Animation::repeat_synced) so that several indicators pulse together. The result is a
closure, so call it as `pulsating_between(min, max)`.

# Examples

```
use gpui::pulsating_between;

let breathe = pulsating_between(0.3, 1.0);
assert!((breathe(0.25) - 1.0).abs() < 1e-6);
assert!((breathe(0.75) - 0.3).abs() < 1e-6);
assert!((breathe(0.0) - 0.65).abs() < 1e-6);
```

Creates a [`Pixels`] length, the usual way to write a fixed size in logical pixels.

# Examples

```
use gpui::{Pixels, px};
assert_eq!(px(12.), Pixels::from(12.));
```

Accelerates from rest. Returns `delta * delta`.

# Examples

```
use gpui::quadratic;

assert_eq!(quadratic(0.5), 0.25);
```

Creates an empty queue and returns its sender and receiver.

# Examples

```
# use gpui::{Priority, queue::PriorityQueueReceiver};
let (sender, mut receiver) = PriorityQueueReceiver::new();
sender.send(Priority::High, 1).unwrap();
assert_eq!(receiver.pop().unwrap(), 1);

// Once the sender is dropped and the queue is empty, `pop` fails.
drop(sender);
assert!(receiver.pop().is_err());
```

Creates a [`Radians`] angle from a value in radians.

# Examples

```
use gpui::radians;
assert_eq!(radians(1.5).0, 1.5);
```

Creates a [`Relative`] length, a fraction of the reference length.

A fraction of `1.0` is the full reference length. Values outside `0.0` to `1.0` give lengths
below zero or above the full reference length.

# Examples

```
use gpui::{AbsoluteLength, px, relative};

let full = relative(1.);
assert_eq!(full.as_f32(), 1.0);
assert_eq!(full.to_pixels(AbsoluteLength::Pixels(px(200.)), px(16.)), px(200.));
```

